// ============================================================================
// CONFIG-RESOLUTION · FRAMEWORK-DRIVEN ENTRY POINTS
//
// call-edge-generation/call_chain.dl answers the STRUCTURAL question: a module
// nothing imports is a root, and so is anything it exports. This file answers the
// FRAMEWORK one — a function nothing in the repository calls, because the thing that
// calls it is a router or a test runner.
//
// Same relation and same contract as java/ and python/engine/config-resolution:
// entry_point(Method, Kind). The kind is carried because the caller matters: a
// consumer asking "is this dead in production?" must be able to exclude what is
// reachable only from tests, and a single kind would make that unanswerable.
//
// ── WHY THIS IS A SEPARATE PHASE AND NOT PART OF resolution/ ────────────────
// Different evidence: a name a framework chose, not a type. Different soundness
// contract: resolution over-approximates into a sound superset, while this layer must
// decline rather than guess -- a wrong root makes dead code look alive, which is the
// dangerous direction. And different churn: these lists move when frameworks move,
// and the type rules must not be touched when Express releases.
// ============================================================================

// ── a path-shaped string argument at a call ─────────────────────────────────
// The discriminator that makes ts_http_route_verb usable. `get`, `set`, `delete`,
// `head` and `options` are ordinary method names all over this ecosystem -- Map, Set,
// Headers, Reflect, every cache -- so the verb alone would mark an enormous number of
// non-routes. A route registration names a PATH, and a path begins with `/`.
//
// literalType is required, not just a non-empty literalValue: that column is the
// parser's general name column and holds the identifier for an IDENTIFIER_REFERENCE,
// so reading it unguarded turns variables into strings.
cfg_path_arg(ce) :- expr_child("client", ce, "ARGUMENT", _, a),
    expr_literal_type("client", "STRING", a),
    expr_literal_value("client", v, a),
    strlen(v) >= 1,
    substr(v, 0, 1) = "/".

// ── a NAMED function passed by reference as an argument ─────────────────────
// `callback_arg` holds only for an ARROW_FUNCTION or FUNCTION_EXPRESSION written at
// the site, because it reads `expr_anon_decl` -- the declaration an expression
// INTRODUCES. A handler that was declared elsewhere and passed by name introduces
// nothing, so it matched neither rule below and a route registration linked or did
// not link purely on whether the author inlined the handler (#1110).
//
// The evidence was already there: the parser resolves the identifier and records
// `referencedEntityKind=METHOD` with the method's own hash, which is a real FK. This
// reads it. The kind guard is what keeps it honest -- an ARGUMENT that is a METHOD
// reference is a callable being handed over, while a VARIABLE or a PARAMETER holding
// a function is a value the binder could not follow and is left to resolution/.
//
// It adds no root of its own. Both callers below keep their existing guards, so a
// named handler is admitted exactly where an inline one already was, and nowhere
// else -- which is the contract this layer is under: decline rather than guess.
cfg_named_fn_arg(ce, argpos, m) :- expr_child("client", ce, "ARGUMENT", argpos, a),
    expr_kind("client", "IDENTIFIER_REFERENCE", _, a),
    expr_referenced("client", "METHOD", m, a).

// ── http: a handler passed to a route registration ──────────────────────────
// `app.get('/api/orders/:id', handler)` — the handler is an argument, so it is
// reached through callback_arg rather than through an attach point. Both guards
// apply: the callee is named after an HTTP method AND the site names a path.
entry_point(m, "http") :- call_site("client", _, cn, _, _, ce, _),
    ts_http_route_verb(cn),
    cfg_path_arg(ce),
    callback_arg(ce, _, m).

entry_point(m, "http") :- call_site("client", _, cn, _, _, ce, _),
    ts_http_route_verb(cn),
    cfg_path_arg(ce),
    cfg_named_fn_arg(ce, _, m).

// ── test: a body the runner invokes ─────────────────────────────────────────
// No path guard is possible here and none is needed: the discriminator is that the
// argument IS a function. `test(x)` where `test` is a variable holding a value
// matches nothing, because callback_arg only holds for an ARROW_FUNCTION or a
// FUNCTION_EXPRESSION written at the site.
//
// This is what connects a library's own suite to the library. On zustand the 25
// existing roots are test MODULES whose bodies call `it(cb)`; without this the
// closure stops at the module body, which is why it reached one declaration.
entry_point(m, "test") :- call_site("client", _, cn, _, _, ce, _),
    ts_test_registrar(cn),
    callback_arg(ce, _, m).

entry_point(m, "test") :- call_site("client", _, cn, _, _, ce, _),
    ts_test_registrar(cn),
    cfg_named_fn_arg(ce, _, m).

// ── NOT COVERED YET, stated rather than left to be discovered ───────────────
//
// A CALLABLE REACHED THROUGH AN OBJECT PROPERTY VALUE: a handler map
// (`registerAll({ '/orders': handleOrder })`), a service implementation object
// (`server.addService(def, { sayHello: impl })`), an options object holding
// callbacks. The parser resolves these the same way it resolves an argument --
// `edgeRole=OBJECT_PROPERTY_VALUE` with `referencedEntityKind=METHOD` -- so the
// evidence is present and the join is one rule away.
//
// It is not written because there is no discriminator. The two rules above are
// admitted by a verb AND a path, or by a registrar name AND the argument being a
// function. An arbitrary call taking an object of functions has neither, and reading
// it would make every options object a source of roots. A wrong root makes dead code
// look alive, which is the direction this layer must not fail in. A cap on how many
// declarations one key may name -- the shape the Java and Python sides settled on --
// is the likely discriminator, and it needs measuring before it is written.
//
// FILE-BASED ROUTERS (Next.js `app/`, SvelteKit, Remix): the default export of a file
// at a conventional PATH is a handler. Nothing here reads a file's path as a
// convention, and doing so needs a project-layout fact the IR does not carry.
//
// (Decorators and a package's published API were listed here; both are covered
// below.)

// ── package_export: what package.json PUBLISHES (#847) ──────────────────────
// `entry_module` is the structural stand-in for "the package's entry": a module
// nothing imports. It misses exactly the case that matters, a library whose index.ts
// is imported by its own tests -- so the library's whole public API, whose caller is
// a consumer outside the repository, read as unreachable, and unreachable reads as
// safe to delete.
//
// ts_package_entry names the published modules directly: the parser reads main,
// module, exports, types and source, and maps each build-output target back to the
// walked source it compiles from; a subpath pattern (`"./*": "./esm/*.mjs"`) is expanded
// to the walked modules it publishes, one path segment per `*`. A named export of such a
// module is a root, under
// the same two guards exported_from_entry_module applies: a real name, and METHOD.
//
// Declined, rather than guessed:
//   - DEFAULT_INDEX. A package.json naming no entry has not said what it publishes;
//     Node's `index.js` default is a fact about loading, not a declaration of an API.
//   - any outcome without a module hash (a pattern row as written, a blocked subpath,
//     a target no walked source answers).
//   - a module that is ALREADY an entry_module, whose exports are rooted by
//     exported_from_entry_module; one root, one kind.
cfg_published_module(mod) :- package_entry("client", _, _, _, src, _, out, mod),
    mod != "",
    src != "DEFAULT_INDEX",
    (out = "RESOLVED" ; out = "RESOLVED_FROM_BUILD_OUTPUT" ; out = "RESOLVED_FROM_PATTERN").

entry_point(m, "package_export") :- cfg_published_module(mod),
    !entry_module(mod),
    module_export_any("client", mod, en, "METHOD", m),
    en != "",
    !cfg_placeholder_name(en).

// `export const createStore = (init) => { … }` is how most of this ecosystem writes a
// published function: the export names a VARIABLE, and the callable is the arrow bound
// to it. Only a variable the parser linked to a function (var_bound_function) — a
// variable holding any other value is not a callable and roots nothing.
entry_point(m, "package_export") :- cfg_published_module(mod),
    !entry_module(mod),
    module_export_any("client", mod, en, "VARIABLE", v),
    var_bound_function("client", m, v),
    en != "",
    !cfg_placeholder_name(en).

// ── …and the other ways a library publishes callable code (#1626) ───────────
// An exported function or arrow const is one shape of a published API. The rest are just
// as common, and each left its code reading as safe to delete:
//   * `export class Store { get() {} static create() {} }` — the consumer calls the
//     class's members, constructor included, and every member it inherits;
//   * `export const produce = engine.produce`, `= engine.finish.bind(engine)`,
//     `= Engine.make` — a const bound to a method REFERENCE, the headline API of
//     libraries that publish one configured instance;
//   * `export const api = { get() {}, put: () => {} }` — an object literal of callables;
//   * `export namespace Result { export function ok() {} }` — a namespace's exported
//     functions;
//   * `export default (x) => …` — an anonymous default arrow or function expression.
//
// Declined, rather than guessed: `private` and `#name` members (no consumer can name
// them), a namespace function without `export`, a method no published value names, and a
// published const holding anything that is not a function.
cfg_package_module(mod) :- cfg_published_module(mod), !entry_module(mod).

cfg_published_export(mod, en, k, e) :- cfg_package_module(mod),
    module_export_any("client", mod, en, k, e),
    en != "",
    !cfg_placeholder_name(en).

// a published class, and every class it inherits from: `store.inherited()` is a call too
cfg_published_class(t) :- cfg_published_export(_, _, "TYPE", t), class_type(t).
cfg_published_class(anc) :- cfg_published_class(t), type_ancestor(t, anc), class_type(anc).

cfg_member_hidden("PRIVATE_ACCESS").
cfg_member_hidden("PRIVATE_NAME_ACCESS").

entry_point(m, "package_export") :- cfg_published_class(t),
    method_owner("client", t, m),
    method_access("client", acc, m),
    !cfg_member_hidden(acc).

entry_point(m, "package_export") :- cfg_published_export(_, _, "TYPE", ns),
    type_decl("client", _, _, _, "NAMESPACE_TYPE", _, ns),
    method_owner("client", ns, m),
    method_access("client", "EXPORTED_ACCESS", m).

// A value the package hands out: a published const's initializer (and each branch it may
// evaluate to), the expression of `export default <expr>`, and the receiver of a `.bind`.
cfg_published_value(x) :- cfg_published_export(_, _, "VARIABLE", v),
    var_initializer("client", _, e, v),
    e != "",
    value_branch(e, x).
cfg_published_value(e) :- cfg_package_module(mod),
    export_decl(_, "default", _, "DEFAULT_EXPRESSION", mod, h),
    export_expression("client", e, h).
cfg_published_value(r) :- cfg_published_value(x),
    expr_kind("client", "CALL_EXPRESSION", _, x),
    call_callee_expr(_, x, ne),
    property_access_name(ne, "bind"),
    expr_child("client", ne, "RECEIVER", _, r).

// the function it is: a written arrow or function expression, a named function, a static
// method read as a value (`Engine.make`) — a call node carries its CALLEE as its
// referenced entity, so a call is not the function it names
entry_point(m, "package_export") :- cfg_published_value(x),
    !expr_kind("client", "CALL_EXPRESSION", _, x),
    expr_callable(x, m),
    method_prov(m, "client").
// an instance method read as a value: `engine.produce`, typed through its receiver
entry_point(m, "package_export") :- cfg_published_value(x),
    property_access_recv(x, n, q),
    expr_type(q, _, t),
    method_in_scope(t, n, _, m),
    method_prov(m, "client").
// the callables of a published object literal
entry_point(m, "package_export") :- cfg_published_value(x),
    objlit_member_fn(x, _, m).
// and what a published const holds by any route the holder rules follow: a function
// another holder hands over, or one a call returns (`export const store = create()`)
entry_point(m, "package_export") :- cfg_published_export(_, _, "VARIABLE", v),
    holder_holds_function(v, m),
    method_prov(m, "client").

// ── DECORATOR-DRIVEN ROOTS (Nest, Angular) ──────────────────────────────────
// The gap the section above described, now closed. projections/decorators.dl gives
// annotation_on the same shape Java and Python already carry, so these three rules
// are the annotation-driven rules from java/engine/config-resolution, ported.
//
// EVERY RULE IS GUARDED BY THE CLASS DECORATOR, and that is the whole soundness
// argument. A decorator name on its own is a name anyone may use: a project is free
// to write its own `@Get` or a method called `ngOnInit`. What is not accidental is the
// PAIR — a route decorator on a method of a class the container owns. Requiring both
// is what keeps this layer declining rather than guessing, which is the contract
// stated at the top of this file.

// ── http: a method carrying a route decorator, inside a container-owned class ──
// `@Controller('/orders') class C { @Get(':id') getOne() {} }`. Nothing calls getOne;
// the router does, by reading metadata the decorator attached.
entry_point(m, "http") :- annotation_on("client", dn, _, "METHOD_DECLARATION", m, _),
    ts_route_decorator(dn),
    method_owner("client", owner, m),
    annotation_on("client", cn, _, "CLASS_DECLARATION", owner, _),
    ts_framework_class_decorator(cn).

// ── bean_ctor: the container constructs the class, so its constructor is a root ──
// Written as two rules rather than one with a disjunction because a class that
// declares no constructor gets a DEFAULT_CONSTRUCTOR, and it is the one that must be
// a root for an injected dependency to be reachable at all. Missing it would leave
// every no-argument service dead.
entry_point(m, "bean_ctor") :- annotation_on("client", cn, _, "CLASS_DECLARATION", t, _),
    ts_framework_class_decorator(cn),
    method_owner("client", t, m),
    method_kind("client", "CONSTRUCTOR", _, m).
entry_point(m, "bean_ctor") :- annotation_on("client", cn, _, "CLASS_DECLARATION", t, _),
    ts_framework_class_decorator(cn),
    method_owner("client", t, m),
    method_kind("client", "DEFAULT_CONSTRUCTOR", _, m).

// ── lifecycle: a hook the container calls by name, with no call site anywhere ──
entry_point(m, "lifecycle") :- annotation_on("client", cn, _, "CLASS_DECLARATION", t, _),
    ts_framework_class_decorator(cn),
    method_owner("client", t, m),
    method_decl("client", name, _, _, m),
    ts_framework_lifecycle_method(name).
